# Units masked on tib, one per line. Mask, not disable: a disabled unit can still be started by a
# socket, a timer or a dependency. Units that do not exist on the board are skipped.
#
# Armbian conveniences with no job on a headless pedal. armbian-zram-config makes a 998 MB
# compressed swap on a 2 GB board, and compression is CPU and memory-bandwidth work, which is what
# the render core shares with everything else.
armbian-hardware-monitor.service
armbian-hardware-optimize.service
armbian-led-state.service
armbian-zram-config.service
# Package maintenance that wakes up on its own. Updates happen when you choose, not mid-gig.
apt-daily.service
apt-daily.timer
apt-daily-upgrade.service
apt-daily-upgrade.timer
man-db.timer
dpkg-db-backup.timer
# e2scrub checks LVM snapshots; this board has no LVM.
e2scrub_all.timer
e2scrub_all.service
# Boot and logging. Logs go to the journal, in RAM.
systemd-networkd-wait-online.service
rsyslog.service
cron.service
#
# Deliberately NOT masked:
#   armbian-ramlog         keeps /var/log in RAM; masking it moves log writes onto the SD card.
#   fake-hwclock-save      hourly; it is what gives the clock a sane start after a power cut.
#   logrotate, systemd-tmpfiles-clean
#                          daily and brief; without them /var/log (50 MB of RAM) and /tmp fill.
#   fstrim                 weekly (Monday ~01:30); keeps the SD card's write speed up.
#   systemd-timesyncd      the only time source.
#   iwd, NetworkManager, wpa_supplicant.service
#                          redundant (systemd-networkd and netplan's wpa_supplicant run the WiFi),
#                          but WiFi is the only way into tib, so these wait for a boot-time
#                          fallback. See devices/tib/README.md.
